## Separate work

### Shared parser: month and day swapped

The shared parser reads month first, including for tenants whose locale is day
first. R-41200 reports 7 April becoming 4 July; R-41201 corrects the direction
of its own example. The tenant directory, not the browser locale, determines the
expected order. Intake tags are guesses, not ground truth.

Fix the shared parser to honour the tenant's declared date convention, or require
an unambiguous structured date. Reproduce with day-first tenants over app and
email paths and booking-v2 form submissions. Add regressions for ambiguous and
unambiguous dates; do not silently accept an invalid day/month combination.

### booking-v3 hint: the UI tells users the wrong order

The booking-v3 field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, while the form reads the tenant's day-first
order. This is not fixed merely by changing the shared parser. R-41230 is a form
report from copper.studio, whose tenant locale is es-ES and form is booking-v3.
R-41241 uses an en-US browser, but its tenant alder.physio is de-DE and booking-v3.

Generate the hint and validation from the same tenant date convention. Test
copper.studio, alder.physio and pale.harbour, including a browser locale that
disagrees with the tenant. Users following the printed hint must get the date
they were told to enter.

### One-day shifts and premature delivery: investigate separately

R-41202 describes 12 May versus 11 May, R-41209 a June-only one-day shift,
R-41344 different days in Lisbon and Toronto, and R-41620 Tuesday becoming Monday.
R-41236 says an invite arrived before the booking existed. These symptoms do not
demonstrate month/day transposition. The existing `causeOf` function files them
under parser solely from tenant and channel; that classification is not proof.

Keep these reports visible in a separate investigation. Timezone conversion,
date-only serialisation, DST and delivery-event ordering are hypotheses to test,
not established root causes. Obtain original timestamps, timezone identifiers,
booking and delivery event IDs, then reproduce. Split this investigation again
if the evidence shows different causes. Never count these IDs twice.

## Why the merge rule failed

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merged on normalised subjects
without reading the body. A shared title does not establish a shared cause, and
different titles can describe the same cause. Tenant locale, booking-form version,
entry channel and a reproducible failure distinguish the paths.

The existing explorer below already disagrees with the old prose: the same tenant
can have different causes through form versus app, and different subjects can
share a cause. An unknown tenant needs investigation, not an automatic merge.
The explorer is a diagnostic aid, not an oracle for the exceptional report bodies.

<DedupeExplorer />

Follow [replacement decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-evidence).
Do not edit accepted decision 0001 to erase the historical policy.

## Received is not merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived from April, but many were merged together in July. A merge spike
shows desk processing, not necessarily when the product broke. Compare
`receivedAt` and `mergedAt` before dating an incident. This chart's date window is
the original export window; it does not show later live reports.

## Status

These are tracked problems, not shipped fixes. No deployment or resolution is
demonstrated by this export. Issue links and evidence are listed in the repository
README. Keep reports separate until a shared mechanism is established.

